.TH CCOL_EVENT_LOOP_SHUTDOWN 3 "2026-07-12" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
ccol_event_loop_shutdown \- stop an event loop's poller and dispatch worker threads
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cthreadcomm.h>

ccol_retval_t ccol_event_loop_shutdown(ccol_event_loop loop);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Tells the poller thread of loop to stop and waits until it has joined
that thread. With num_reactor_threads > 1 it then drains and joins every
dispatch worker thread as well. The drain is graceful: every job that
is already queued or running finishes, and nothing is cancelled. Either
way, no dispatch can be in flight once the call returns.

When nothing of loop dispatches any more, each of its queue
registrations stops taking wakes from its queue, and a wake that it was
holding goes on to the next listener of that queue, so the other
listeners of a queue that loop watched keep getting every message. The
registrations themselves stay in place until ccol_event_loop_remove(3)
or ccol_event_loop_destroy(3), and their on_removed still runs then.
Any later ccol_event_loop_add(3) on loop is refused.

The call is idempotent, so it is safe to make it several times and from
several threads at once. Exactly one caller does the joins, and every
other caller waits until those joins are done before it returns, so
each caller gets its "blocks until joined" guarantee whichever one
started the shutdown.

ccol_event_loop_destroy() runs the shutdown itself if nobody called
this function, so calling it first is optional. Call it directly when
stopping the threads and freeing the resources have to happen at
different times.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
loop  ccol_event_loop to shut down.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
ccol_success        The loop is shut down, by this call or an earlier
                    one.
ccol_invalid_args   loop is CCOL_EVENT_LOOP_INVALID, a stale handle,
                    or a handle that has already been destroyed.
ccol_not_permitted  The call comes from inside a callback that runs on
                    one of the threads of loop (the poller, or a
                    dispatch worker). See WARNINGS.
.fi
.SH WARNINGS
.nf
Do not call this function from a callback that runs on one of loop's
threads. The call would join that thread from itself, which is
undefined behavior and gives EDEADLK. It joins the poller thread and,
with num_reactor_threads > 1, also drains and joins every dispatch
worker, which is where the callbacks run in that case. The library
detects such a call and refuses it with ccol_not_permitted rather than
leaving undefined behavior for a caller to trigger. Hand the shutdown
to another thread, or do it after the callback returns.
.fi
.SH THREAD SAFETY
.nf
Safe to call from several threads at the same time, as long as none
of them is one of loop's own threads: the poller thread and, with
num_reactor_threads > 1, the dispatch worker threads.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR ccol_event_loop_destroy (3),
.BR ccol_event_loop_create (3)
